Well, if you want an alternative (and I think more likely) reading of
the tea leaves, I think that even with the glacial pace of patches
(let alone enhancements) to NetHack in the 21st century, the lack of a
patch addressing farming since that time is a concrete indication that
whatever one guy felt at one point in time wasn't compelling enough to
cause a change. A fix is probably 10 lines of code at most so there is
no inherent difficulty involved in a patch. There are many approaches
to implementing the patch, none of which are difficult to conceive.

I don't know why people can't accept farming and move on. Do it or
don't do it, for crying out loud. But the argument comes up among
*players* in every single game where farming is possible, which is a
huge variety of games. The fact is, it remains possible because, on
the whole, the flexibility in play that it offers outweighs whatever
unseemliness it creates.

"Schadenfreude" is a word meaning "taking pleasure in the misery of
others." It is the alternate face of "being jealous of the enjoyment
of others." Both of these are compelling, and base, motivations for
"fixing" the game. It is JUST A GAME. People play it to enjoy it.

Anyway, back to something worthwhile.



